fix[DeepPartial, DeepReadonly]: preserve branded primitive types

## Summary

This PR follows up on the brand-preservation regression item listed under “Worth doing regardless” in [#1986](https://github.com/toss/es-toolkit/issues/1986).

`DeepPartial` and `DeepReadonly` recursively transform object property values. When a plain object contains a branded primitive field, that field is processed through the object branch and is no longer equivalent to the original branded type.

```ts
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };

type User = {
  id: UserId;
};

type PartialUser = DeepPartial<User>;
// Expected: { id?: UserId }

type ReadonlyUser = DeepReadonly<User>;
// Expected: { readonly id: UserId }
```

The shared `Primitive` type introduced in [[#2044](https://github.com/toss/es-toolkit/pull/2044)](https://github.com/toss/es-toolkit/pull/2044) provides a common terminal condition for these recursive utilities. Primitive types, including branded primitive types, can therefore pass through without being recursively mapped as objects.

## Changes

- Use the existing `Primitive` type as a terminal condition in `DeepPartial` and `DeepReadonly`.
- Add type-level regression tests covering both direct and nested branded primitive inputs.
- Replace the explicit `any[]` function constraints with `never[]`, preserving parameterized function types without lint warnings.
- Update the related reference documentation in English, Korean, Japanese, and Simplified Chinese.

## Implementation notes

This is a type-only change and does not affect runtime behavior. Reusing the existing `Primitive` type avoids duplicating the primitive union across both utilities.

The `never[]` function constraint still matches arbitrary function signatures in the conditional type while avoiding an explicit `any`.
